# Mutation efficacy per package: the share of mutants the tests kill, of the ones they could.
#
# A floor is where the package is today, and it may only move up — same rule as .coveragefloors.
# A package not listed here is reported and not enforced, because a floor invented before the
# first measurement is a number nobody chose.
#
# Efficacy answers the question coverage cannot: the line ran, but would a test have failed if it
# were wrong?
#
# Two things that move the number without anything getting worse:
#
#   A mutant killed by the runner's patience (a loop that never ends) is reported as timed out and
#   counted neither way, so a package with one moves by up to a point between runs.
#
#   A test that covers new code brings *new* mutants into the denominator. Efficacy is killed over
#   reachable mutants, so adding a test can lower it — that is coverage improving, not assertions
#   weakening. The floor tolerates the dip; the number to watch is killed mutants, which only rises.
#
# A mutant killed by the runner's patience — a loop that never ends — is reported as timed out and
# not counted either way, so a package with one moves by up to a point between runs. Floors sit
# below the lowest observed number, not on it.
#
# Measured 2026-10-27, worst first. `sandbox` at 100 means every mutation in its covered code is
# caught, which is what a pass over the engine should leave behind.
internal/control     88
internal/sandbox    100
internal/supervise   87
internal/update      87
internal/vault       88
internal/workerpool  87
internal/ready       86
#
# Not measured, on purpose: `internal/httpapi` and `cmd/acornfall` keep their tests in the
# end-to-end suite, and gremlins only runs the package's own. A number from them would report the
# absence of unit tests, not the strength of the tests. Their confidence signal is coverage through
# the feature suite, and `make cover` says it.
